  "response": "Whether a large language model has acquired a reasoning procedure or is reprising text it has already seen is treated as an empirical question, and it is attacked by building evaluations that mere recall cannot survive. GSM1k was constructed by Scale AI as a held-out replica of GSM8K to measure benchmark contamination, and that purpose-built replica status confirms that the benchmark was released by DeepMind in 2022. GSM-Symbolic, developed after that held-out replica, generates variants of benchmark problems by substituting new names and numeric values into templates. Because such substitution leaves the required inferential steps intact while altering every surface cue, it drove model accuracy on the perturbed problem variants below accuracy on the original benchmark. The one remaining possibility, namely that accuracy on the perturbed variants remained at least as high as accuracy on the original benchmark, excludes that observed drop and is excluded by it, so exactly one of the two must hold of any given system.\n\nThe fall-off under perturbation is what carries the diagnostic weight: it shows that the test set items were present in the model's pretraining corpus; in other words, the model's pretraining data included the items that make up the test set. A system that had internalized the procedure would be indifferent to a change of names and digits, whereas one that had indexed strings could not be. Inference from behaviour is nevertheless indirect, and it is therefore paired with audits of the corpus itself. Researchers compute n-gram overlap between benchmark items and pretraining corpora to detect contamination, flagging any evaluation example whose wording recurs verbatim in the training text.\n\nThe thresholds used for such flagging are themselves contested. The GPT-3 paper used a 13-gram overlap criterion to flag contaminated test examples, and it used an 8-gram overlap criterion to flag them; one and the same filtering procedure cannot have been governed by both window lengths, so the two descriptions cannot both be true. Settling which figure is correct matters in practice, since the stringency of the window determines how much of a benchmark is declared compromised and how much of a reported score survives.\n\nNor can a model's own account of its work serve as a tiebreaker. Turpin and colleagues demonstrated that chain-of-thought explanations can be unfaithful to the factors that actually drive a model's answer. Whereas that line of inquiry must take the model's narration at face value, a different tradition sets narration aside and intervenes directly on the computation that produces the output. Mechanistic interpretability uses activation patching to identify the causal circuit responsible for a model's output, and circuits recovered in this way show that transformers trained on modular addition implement a Fourier-based trigonometric algorithm, as Nanda and colleagues found. The conclusion recorded by Nanda and colleagues is that modular addition transformers store a memorized lookup table of sums. No single probe is decisive on its own; the verdict rests on the convergence of perturbation tests, contamination audits, and circuit-level analysis.",
